              text: "## Summary of Q4 2025 Search Findings\n\n### What is a Fiscal Quarter?\nA fiscal quarter is a **three-month period** used by companies for financial reporting. Q4 specifically covers **October, November, and December** in a standard calendar-year fiscal cycle. Public companies are required by the SEC to file quarterly reports (Form 10-Q) four times per year.\n\n---\n\n### Key Q4 2025 Business & Economic Highlights\n\n**Banking & Finance**\n- FDIC-insured institutions reported **net income of $77.7 billion** (ROA of 1.24%), a slight 2% decrease from Q3 2025\n- Full-year 2025 bank net income reached **$295.6 billion**, up 10.2% from 2024\n- Mastercard saw strong growth with gross dollar volume **up 7%**, cross-border volume **up 14%**, and switched transactions **up 10%** year-over-year\n\n**Consumer & Retail**\n- Consumers remained **cautious**, shifting spending toward essentials, discounts, and private-label products\n- Consumer M&A activity **fell ~22% quarter-over-quarter** to a two-year low\n- Brands increasingly adopted **AI-driven personalization** and omnichannel strategies\n\n**Notable Company Results**\n- **IBM** delivered strong Q4 revenue with double-digit software and infrastructure growth; generative AI bookings surpassed **$12.5 billion**\n- **ABB** exceeded **$10 billion in orders** for the first time in a single quarter, with record annual free cash flow of $4.6 billion\n- **Ondas Inc.** reported record Q4 revenue of **$30.1 million**, representing ~605% full-year growth\n- **Kratos Defense** grew Q4 revenues **20% organically** to $345.1 million, with a record backlog of $1.57 billion\n- **Warrior Met Coal** posted record Q4 sales volumes of 2.9 million short tons, with revenue up 29% year-over-year\n\n**Private Markets**\n- U.S. direct lending volumes reached **$380 billion** for full-year 2025\n- Real estate recovery **stalled**, with core real estate posting negative appreciation for the second straight quarter\n\n**Cannabis**\n- Only **7 M&A transactions** in Q4, the lowest in two years, despite Trump's move to fast-track federal rescheduling to Schedule III\n\n---\n\n### Looking Ahead to 2026\nAnalysts project **strong earnings growth** continuing into 2026, with S&P 500 companies expected to report year-over-year earnings growth of 23–27% through Q3 and Q4 2026, driven largely by the Energy and Technology sectors.",
